No – there really isn't usable room on that side. The zone immediately to the left of the seated pair (the man in the black T‑shirt and the woman in the grey shirt facing him) is already taken up by:

- A bar‑height (high‑top) table on a pedestal base, with a wooden stool pulled up beside it;
- The duffel bag lying on the tiles next to the woman's chair;
- The customer queue/walkway that runs along the pastry display case and service counter, where several people are standing in line.

The clear floor between the high‑top table's base and the couple's chairs is only a narrow strip – well under a meter – while a low round table with two chairs needs roughly a 1.2–1.5 m square footprint once the chairs are pulled out. The only larger open patch of tile on that side is the main circulation aisle/queue lane in front of the counter, so planting a table there would block traffic to the register and pickup area.

In short, the left side is functionally committed to the high‑top table, the stool, and pedestrian flow; adding another two‑chair round table there would crowd the seated pair and obstruct the walkway, so there isn't enough room. (The empty floor space for additional low round tables is on the right‑hand side of the room, where the existing round tables and black chairs already sit.)
